{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1873 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0c40313080be744e7165e60c564f1abbfa11004c8220afcdd9a585bca3085a07" :edb/db-before-t 1872 :edb/db-after-t 1873 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535185 50 #inst "2026-10-13T02:57:10.270Z" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054393 1071 "Evaluate $11p-2$ for $p=4$." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186054393 1071 "Evaluate $11p-2$ for $p=4.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054393 1074 "Replacing $p$ with $\\color{blue}4$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 11p-2 \\\\ &= 11({\\color{blue}4})-2 \\\\ &= 44-2 \\\\ &= 42. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186054393 1074 "Replacing $p$ with $\\color{blue}4$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t11p-2 &= \\\n\t\t11({\\color{blue}4})-2 &= \\\n\t \t44-2 &= \\\n\t \t42 .\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054394 1071 "Evaluate $2(3 x-1) +2$ for $x=-3$." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186054394 1071 "Evaluate $2(3 x-1) +2$ for $x=-3.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054394 1074 "We replace $x$ with $-3$ in the expression and evaluate the parentheses first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(3x - 1) + 2 &=  \\\\\n2(3 \\cdot (-3) - 1) + 2 &=  \\\\\n2(-9 - 1) + 2 &=  \\\\\n2(-10) + 2 &=  \\\\\n- 20 + 2 &=  \\\\\n&= - 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186054394 1074 "We replace $x$ with $-3$ in the expression and evaluate the parentheses first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3x-1)+2 & = \\\\ 2(3\\cdot (-3)-1)+2 & = \\\\ 2(-9-1)+2 & = \\\\ 2(-10)+2 & = \\\\ -20+2 & = \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054395 1071 "Find the value of $x - \\dfrac{3}{2}y$ when $x=2$ and $y=-2$." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186054395 1071 "Find the value of $x - \\dfrac{3}{2}y$ when $x=2$ and $y=-2.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054395 1074 "Replacing $x$ with $2$ and $y$ with $-2$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - \\frac{3}{2}y &=  \\\\\n2 - \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot (-2) &=  \\\\\n2 + (-\\frac{3}{2}) \\cdot (-2) &=  \\\\\n2 + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186054395 1074 "Replacing $x$ with $2$ and $y$ with $-2$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{3}{2}y & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-2) & = \\\\ 2+(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (-2) & = \\\\ 2+3 & = \\\\ 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054424 1071 "Evaluate $2-\\dfrac{u}{4}$ for $u=\\dfrac43$." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186054424 1071 "Evaluate $2-\\dfrac{u}{4}$ for $u=\\dfrac43.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054424 1074 "Replacing $u$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac43\n$$\n\nin the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 - \\frac{u}{4} &=  \\\\\n2 - \\frac{1}{4}u &=  \\\\\n2 - \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} &=  \\\\\n2 - \\frac{4}{12} &=  \\\\\n2 - \\frac{1}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{6 - 1}{3} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186054424 1074 "Replacing $u$ with $\\dfrac43$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{u}{4} & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{1}{4}u & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{4}{12} & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{1}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{6-1}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479062 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479123 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479069 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479111 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479081 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479109 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479068 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479125 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186054916 1050 540.0 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186056016 1064 "To **evaluate** the expression $3+x$ for\n\n$$\nx={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\n, we replace $x$ with $\\color{blue}2$ in the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + x &=  \\\\\n3 + (2) &=  \\\\\n3 + 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186056016 1064 "To **evaluate** the expression $3+x$ for $x={\\color{blue}2}$, we replace $x$ with $\\color{blue}2$ in the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+x & = \\\\ 3+(2) & = \\\\ 3+2 & = \\\\ 5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186056017 1064 "The usual order of operations (PEMDAS) applies when evaluating expressions. So when an algebraic expression contains parentheses $(\\ldots)$, we calculate what's inside the parentheses *first*.\n\nFor example, we evaluate $6 - (2x+3)$ at $x=3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 - (2x + 3) &=  \\\\\n6 - (2 \\cdot 3 + 3) &=  \\\\\n6 - (6 + 3) &=  \\\\\n6 - 9 &=  \\\\\n&= - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186056017 1064 "The usual order of operations (PEMDAS) applies when evaluating expressions. So when an algebraic expression contains parentheses $(\\ldots)$, we calculate what's inside the parentheses *first*. \n\nFor example, we evaluate $6 - (2x+3)$ at $x=3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-(2x+3) & = \\\\ 6-(2\\cdot 3+3) & = \\\\ 6-(6+3) & = \\\\ 6-9 & = \\\\ -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186056018 1064 "If an expression contains more than one variable, we evaluate it using the same procedure, replacing each variable with the appropriate value.\n\nFor example, to find the value of\n\n$$\n2b + \\dfrac{1}{2}a\n$$\n\nwhen $a=-2$ and $b=3$, we replace $a$ with $-2$ and $b$ with $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2b + \\frac{1}{2}a &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3 + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-2) &=  \\\\\n6 - 1 &=  \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186056018 1064 "If an expression contains more than one variable, we evaluate it using the same procedure, replacing each variable with the appropriate value.\n\nFor example, to find the value of $2b + \\dfrac{1}{2}a$ when $a=-2$ and $b=3,$ we replace $a$ with $-2$ and $b$ with $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2b+\\frac{1}{2}a & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2) & = \\\\ 6-1 & = \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186064251 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479082 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186064251 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479119 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186064251 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479132 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186064252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479102 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186064253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479075 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186064253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479089 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186064396 1050 1140.0 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186065447 1064 "Consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x)= 4x^2+x, \\qquad x \\gt 0\n$$\n\nThe domain restriction $x > 0$ means that $f$ is invertible on its entire domain. \n\nThe point $P$ with coordinates $(2,18)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x).$ This means that the point $P'$ with coordinates $(18,2)$ lies on the curve of the inverse function $y=f^{-1}(x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e16e532743be547c766e5a6a47bae8933a8dedb2b6d0fa0d60d47cc0b37ba04.png)\n\nHow do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at the point $P'?$\n\nFor this, we will use the fact that the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at $P'$ is the *reciprocal* of the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $P.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{{P}^{'}}=\\frac{1}{({\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{P})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at $P$ is straightforward. First, we differentiate $y=f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 8x + 1,\n$$\n\nand then plugging in $x=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_P = 8(2)+1 = 17.\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_{P'}= \\dfrac{1}{17}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this process using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\n$$\n\nTo help remember this formula, note that it's okay to swap the derivative symbol (the prime) with the inverse symbol (the exponent of $-1$) as long as we take the reciprocal afterward." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186065447 1064 "Consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x)= 4x^2+x, \\qquad x \\gt 0\n$$\n\nThe domain restriction $x > 0$ means that $f$ is invertible on its entire domain. \n\nThe point $P$ with coordinates $(2,18)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x).$ This means that the point $P'$ with coordinates $(18,2)$ lies on the curve of the inverse function $y=f^{-1}(x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e16e532743be547c766e5a6a47bae8933a8dedb2b6d0fa0d60d47cc0b37ba04.png)\n\nHow do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at the point $P'?$\n\nFor this, we will use the fact that the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at $P'$ is the *reciprocal* of the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $P.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{{P}^{′}}=\\frac{1}{({\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{P})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at $P$ is straightforward. First, we differentiate $y=f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 8x + 1,\n$$\n\nand then plugging in $x=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_P = 8(2)+1 = 17.\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_{P'}= \\dfrac{1}{17}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this process using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\n$$\n\nTo help remember this formula, note that it's okay to swap the derivative symbol (the prime) with the inverse symbol (the exponent of $-1$) as long as we take the reciprocal afterward." 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186065448 1064 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and $f'(x)$ at some points. \n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $12$ |\n\nLet's use this data to find $(f^{-1})'(1).$ \n\nRecall that the formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(1) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(2)=1$, hence $f^{-1}(1)=2$, and also $f'(2)=12$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use the rule for calculating derivatives of inverse functions in conjunction with other rules of differentiation. Let's see an example." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186065448 1064 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and $f'(x)$ at some points. \n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $12$ |\n\nLet's use this data to find $(f^{-1})'(1).$ \n\nRecall that the formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(1) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(2)=1$, hence $f^{-1}(1)=2$, and also $f'(2)=12$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use the rule for calculating derivatives of inverse functions in conjunction with other rules of differentiation. Let's see an example." 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186076274 1078 #edb/ref 17592186076277 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186078610 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479074 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186078612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479140 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186078916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186078916 1071 "What is the coefficient of $y$ in the expression $\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{3y}{4} + \\frac{11z}{8}$?" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186078916 1071 "What is the coefficient of $y$ in the expression $\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{3y}{4}+\\frac{11z}{8}?$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186078916 1074 "The expression has three terms: \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2},\\,-\\frac{3y}{4},\\,\\frac{11z}{8}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe only term with the variable $y$ is $-\\frac{3y}{4},$ so $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is the coefficient of $y.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186094759 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094764 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186095970 1078 #edb/ref 17592186095975 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096804 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479066 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096804 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479118 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096804 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479146 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479073 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479103 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479114 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479077 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479099 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479120 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479076 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479091 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479136 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186096895 1050 1080.0 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186097131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186097131 1071 "![](images/28/28ab65439d4ee144f9928e3b5048045858d4207794f886622b0961893d98615a.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x)$ above, find $h^{′}(2)$ where $h(x) = (f(x))/(g(x))$." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186097131 1071 "![](images/28/28ab65439d4ee144f9928e3b5048045858d4207794f886622b0961893d98615a.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186097131 1074 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(2)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $g(2)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-1)-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 0}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186097341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186097341 1071 "![](images/9e/9ec9b64e62efa9435f5d00126164a01628c86ab16a49f52b478e81a83e5b7d2a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is given above. Find $f^{′}(-1)$." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186097341 1071 "![](images/9e/9ec9b64e62efa9435f5d00126164a01628c86ab16a49f52b478e81a83e5b7d2a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-1).$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186097341 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(-1),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in [-2,0].$ Notice that $x=-1$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/2c/2c01b2337e4ca3a872e9f72ef1b52f0190096203ba2f0a4896cc11a028578d66.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,0)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-(-1)}{0-(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186117409 1078 #edb/ref 17592186117411 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119608 1071 "![](images/91/9129b6fbd360b9b9bcc937fd2acf0aedc25681d4b3dca100be7f681b7bb347dd.png) \n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of $l_{42} \\cdot u_{24}?$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186119608 1071 "![](images/ca/ca0e3d789d60061d6aa1d600db4d604ffa3a1dd2dd5483bf1493d85c8e39cb61.svg) \n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of $l_{42} \\cdot u_{24}?$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119608 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 9 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & \\,2\\, & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n![](images/7e/7ea3d13032296c2ed0c9f447860a7b6ec7f8ff32a9e7dce6b2d7e51d3cee0cd3.svg)\n\nFinally, since $l_{42}=-2$ and $u_{24}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\nl_{42} \\cdot u_{24} = (-2) \\cdot (-3) =6.\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119608 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 9 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & \\,2\\, & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n![](images/ac/ac086686e36fdc3e3fffbf4e772f5bdd3d797a71cd06a8e0c4c0bc338051371e.png)\n\nFinally, since $l_{42}=-2$ and $u_{24}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\nl_{42} \\cdot u_{24} = (-2) \\cdot (-3) =6.\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186119609 1071 "![](images/09/092a8a3d8ca1efa88189085838d4782072a254ac3790cf01f25ad9b5d0bca0ca.png)\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry $l_{31}$ in the matrix $L?$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186119609 1071 "![](images/b7/b74e568ca53f2365641cb8ecd94bf0b03c930736808c395f67733b19164508d1.svg)\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry $l_{31}$ in the matrix $L?$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119610 1071 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b},$ given by\n![](images/45/45cefb7311cd5253b76164cc7f2f3ce01b445f0e9061b720865227128b60debe.png)\nIf $\\langle x_1,x_2 \\rangle$ and $\\langle y_1,y_2 \\rangle$ are the solutions to the systems $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $L\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{b}$ respectively, what is the value of $x_1 \\cdot y_2?$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186119610 1071 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b},$ given by\n![](images/f1/f1124e83e3b7614557a1a6de54523cc99f62ba2a52af1da8e4d22e14466ce020.svg)\nIf $\\langle x_1,x_2 \\rangle$ and $\\langle y_1,y_2 \\rangle$ are the solutions to the systems $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $L\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{b}$ respectively, what is the value of $x_1 \\cdot y_2?$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119610 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/a1/a185a6fd91c78ed3e291b3ac8b99f9430290e5d8c1060a7d97317efc230aeb40.svg)\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/1e/1e780d384ca1fd957d742bbd047399c558ab016d187ce4cf71682546673aba35.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=6 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=6$ and $y_2=4.$ Therefore, \n![](images/58/58da62903247cf6dfdf33be86293c181ef5184048ca7cd6c921360e5d202f353.svg)\n\n- Next, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/e0/e0563dde4944ecbe2ccb98bc91246fa18c7a808df96ab77f9f8efb5510084677.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x_2=-2$ and $x_1=2.$ Therefore, \n![](images/a8/a84088cff2a3589652ef7cabf09e3d8429fad7797ea20d44a416f85dcc7f9b65.svg)\n\nFinally, $x_1 \\cdot y_2 = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119610 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/b5/b521c0b31d05de273d0abd37e00b046c0e36ed47ef30d22784a24cace4d676f9.png)\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/9d/9dcc9f71745cce72ce7e122b3bc8cd7064d05f3be90d712202f42956956ef18e.png)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=6 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=6$ and $y_2=4.$ Therefore, \n![](images/90/903fbfe6828526dace264d65baebf58c31e36db8a54e25ed1f3be859e6079ebb.png)\n\n- Next, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/dc/dcb234ae13cab2676ea5afa7fde452c870ff5088ff5748342c517bf2a96327a8.png)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x_2=-2$ and $x_1=2.$ Therefore, \n![](images/53/53bace542ba4dfd0d7e5e4bf9b6451c7f39502dd1bf24d030f654050629f0e0d.png)\n\nFinally, $x_1 \\cdot y_2 = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186119611 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b,$ given by \n![](images/a1/a13a7752a224bdabc27558a6a41e2b24d3c7918af1ca4d9cff802dcb54259238.svg)\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" 