}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 24 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & \\,1\\, & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/15/150ac38c26af9d8b9dcf60a7f76ae260b293787b0930712eaf175829249f42ff.svg)\n\nFinally, since $l_{31}=-2$ and $u_{23}=-9,$ we get\n\n$$\nl_{31} \\cdot u_{23} = (-2) \\cdot (-9) =18.\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119705 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -12 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 24 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & \\,1\\, & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/1a/1ae6992336eda022f30a643ff0e89ae46c37978ac193370e518ecbea5084878b.png)\n\nFinally, since $l_{31}=-2$ and $u_{23}=-9,$ we get\n\n$$\nl_{31} \\cdot u_{23} = (-2) \\cdot (-9) =18.\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186119706 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b,$ given by \n\n![](images/9d/9d0b186213211813b290f502888f68e0603bc9f77bcd7ea9d6262cb7449c00e7.svg)\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119706 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b,$ given by \n\n![](images/fd/fd1cf7c3d929fb79576a59d4ef3aebd3f24a0d570c938ab80ebd44c5bd4c238f.png)\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186119706 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/42/4298fe244fd6c66c7623ad508c97b96464321387506aa1e4be5e2399a5c9a862.svg)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 66\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=5 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9.$ \n\nTherefore, ![](images/a8/a86dfd0e07b8c415e526764cf9f7ba8f020609bf7b13cc90536deba868ec4aa6.svg)" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119706 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/b5/b521c0b31d05de273d0abd37e00b046c0e36ed47ef30d22784a24cace4d676f9.png)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 66\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=5 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9.$ \n\nTherefore, ![](images/cc/ccb4ab47d3a270cf9545d4f79e6f90b944cf6121fca3752eeee6ddc712fcfdec.png)" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186119797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479055 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479130 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479071 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479116 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479110 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479113 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479090 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479095 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479139 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479067 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479126 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479080 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479056 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479088 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479106 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186119886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479145 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186120003 1050 1020.0 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186120004 1050 660.0 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186120206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117397 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186120208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186120208 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 12 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & \\boxed{} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{32}$ in the matrix $L?$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186120208 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 12 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -12\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & N & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry $l_{32}$ in the matrix $L$?" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186120208 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 12 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{32}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $3$rd row of $A,$ and we add the $2$nd row of $A$ multiplied by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{32}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{2},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}=-(-1)=1.\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186121030 1064 "In general, the LU factorization $A=LU$ of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ is the product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n![](images/65/65ce7b2142905014d090f6f870ad6f98ccc73225316b0dbb385b55ad8f7eb14f.svg)\n\nis a *unit* lower triangular matrix, and\n\n![](images/8d/8d752d8bec624577225a35d276700d568b6ca358b8aad0ace2108c293da7b74a.svg)\n\nis an upper triangular matrix.\n\nAs it turns out, there is a quick way of finding a matrix's LU factorization, which is particularly helpful when factoring large matrices. \n\nLet's see a concrete example of how this works." 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186121030 1064 "In general, the LU factorization $A=LU$ of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ is the product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1 &  & 0 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ⋯ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis a *unit* lower triangular matrix, and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ⋯ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗ &  & ∗ \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis an upper triangular matrix.\n\nAs it turns out, there is a quick way of finding a matrix's LU factorization, which is particularly helpful when factoring large matrices. \n\nLet's see a concrete example of how this works." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186121031 1064 "There is an algorithm that uses $LU$ factorization to solve systems of equations of the form \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation:\n\n![](images/77/77bbf7683a8ecfbd6864ce82eede96dcab60480e9f5395ac38eae88218af8fde.svg)\n\nSince the LU factorization of $A$ is\n\n![](images/d7/d704b00f3e25fdd85fcf123baf0340f87b82125ae2ce0718891102c9da9968f2.svg)\nour equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as\n\n![](images/14/1481be73064ba910f27931cba3f6a252123090c055ed3d4eb3657fd9b9084c58.svg)\n\ni.e., \n\n$$\nLU\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we set $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the above, we have\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, we can reduce the problem to the following system of matrix equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L$ and $U$ are both triangular, each of these equations is easily solvable using back and forward substitution.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Let's assume that ![](images/46/46e89549c82a3571050bf103d9f539cfd82c197c6c0ffef9161ed3c67cdbb4c0.svg) Then, we have\n![](images/af/af1f7463d07d4ddf3f25b8ae1cff3d658f8c0d2a9ab8295c5ea1e0e9c69d2fe6.svg)\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *forward* substitution, we get the solution\n![](images/5b/5bb589d79fbb718f91b5df14ae4c7ac0a3767cb625c2079c0b16562b74484491.svg)\n\n- Finally, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ We have\n![](images/4a/4a54f9bbd8830b3be687a168c8d9548466124669f8e7309ee18e1f05904fd084.svg)\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *back* substitution, we get the solution\n![](images/ba/baf88432261a561613c0241b2d768e9cedf4f4eddaecaaf761b774c36c1ed541.svg)" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186121031 1064 "There is an algorithm that uses $LU$ factorization to solve systems of equations of the form \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the LU factorization of $A$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nour equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ni.e., \n\n$$\nLU\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we set $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the above, we have\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, we can reduce the problem to the following system of matrix equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L$ and $U$ are both triangular, each of these equations is easily solvable using back and forward substitution.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Let's assume that $[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *forward* substitution, we get the solution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Finally, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ We have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *back* substitution, we get the solution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186121114 1064 "Suppose we want to find the LU factorization $A=LU$ of the matrix\n\n![](images/59/59a2c1380bf1b9f6ae162f1a9577082f38f16a8cd7b620700f2ca8738707f0b9.svg)\n\nWe proceed by reducing $A$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination.\n\nTo start, we want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}1}}.$ To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}2}$nd row of $A,$ and we add the ${\\color{red}1}$st row of $A$ multiplied by ${\\color{purple}-3}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We take the factor $\\color{purple}-3$ that we multiplied $R_{\\color{red}1}$ by, change its sign, and write it as the entry $a_{{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}1}}$ with a box around it. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: \n\n- You can think of the box $\\bbox[FloralWhite, 2pt, border-radius: 5px; border: 1px solid black]{\\color{purple}\\:\\phantom{3}\\:}$ as representing the original zero.\n\n- In addition, we store the value $\\color{purple}3$ to use later.\n\n- When doing this in practice, you might find it helpful to write a large zero with a small $\\color{purple}3$ inside it. This is represented by $\\bbox[FloralWhite, 2pt, border-radius: 5px; border: 1px solid black]{\\color{purple}\\:3\\:}$ in our matrix above.\n\nLet's now continue with our Gaussian elimination.\n\nWe want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}.$ To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}3}$rd row of $(\\ast)$ and we add the ${\\color{red}1}$st row multiplied by ${\\color{purple}-2}.$ Then, we place $-({\\color{purple}-2})={\\color{purple}2}$ with a box around it at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ \\,2\\, & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}2}}.$ To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}3}$rd row and we add the ${\\color{red}2}$nd row multiplied by ${\\color{purple}2}.$ Then, we place $-({\\color{purple}2})={\\color{purple}-2}$ with a box around it at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}2}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ \\,2\\, & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ \\,2\\, & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form (remember that the boxes each represent zero), and we're almost done.\n\nNow, here's the neat bit. Both $L$ and $U$ can be extracted from the matrix above:\n\n- The matrix $L$ takes all elements that lie *below* the main diagonal:\n![](images/73/7306c778b7601fef17d1eab6f646736df54b6d80917eab4325969bed47e3880f.svg)\nRemember that $L$ must be *unit* lower triangular.\n\n- The matrix $U$ takes the remaining elements:\n![](images/ff/ff0f5bc3aeb65f931be7435547885ccc975354d20f286ce311771b74d4cce079.svg)\nRemember that $U$ must be upper triangular." 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186121114 1064 "Suppose we want to find the LU factorization $A=LU$ of the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe proceed by reducing $A$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination.\n\nTo start, we want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}1}}.$ To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}2}$nd row of $A,$ and we add the ${\\color{red}1}$st row of $A$ multiplied by ${\\color{purple}-3}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We take the factor $\\color{purple}-3$ that we multiplied $R_{\\color{red}1}$ by, change its sign, and write it as the entry $a_{{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}1}}$ with a box around it. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: \n\n- You can think of the box $\\bbox[FloralWhite, 2pt, border-radius: 5px; border: 1px solid black]{\\color{purple}\\:\\phantom{3}\\:}$ as representing the original zero.\n\n- In addition, we store the value $\\color{purple}3$ to use later.\n\n- When doing this in practice, you might find it helpful to write a large zero with a small $\\color{purple}3$ inside it. This is represented by $\\bbox[FloralWhite, 2pt, border-radius: 5px; border: 1px solid black]{\\color{purple}\\:3\\:}$ in our matrix above.\n\nLet's now continue with our Gaussian elimination.\n\nWe want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}.$ To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}3}$rd row of $(\\ast)$ and we add the ${\\color{red}1}$st row multiplied by ${\\color{purple}-2}.$ Then, we place $-({\\color{purple}-2})={\\color{purple}2}$ with a box around it at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ \\,2\\, & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}2}}.$ To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}3}$rd row and we add the ${\\color{red}2}$nd row multiplied by ${\\color{purple}2}.$ Then, we place $-({\\color{purple}2})={\\color{purple}-2}$ with a box around it at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}2}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ \\,2\\, & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ \\,3\\, & -2 & -2 \\\\ \\,2\\, & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form (remember that the boxes each represent zero), and we're almost done.\n\nNow, here's the neat bit. Both $L$ and $U$ can be extracted from the matrix above:\n\n- The matrix $L$ takes all elements that lie *below* the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRemember that $L$ must be *unit* lower triangular.\n\n- The matrix $U$ takes the remaining elements:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRemember that $U$ must be upper triangular." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186122828 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122829 13194139535185 17592186