n inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-5 + 7) \\div (-2) \\cdot 6 &=  \\\\\n&= 2 \\div (-2) \\cdot 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that if both multiplication and division appear in an expression, we complete each operation from left to right (similarly for addition and subtraction).\nSo, we first divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\div (-2) \\cdot 6 &=  \\\\\n&= - 1 \\cdot 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen finally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 \\cdot 6 &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186153880 1078 #edb/ref 17592186153883 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186157393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479144 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186157395 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479070 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186157395 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479115 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186157606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186157606 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5},\\,0<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20},$ compute $\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186157606 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{3x^{2} + 4}{5}, 0 < x < 1$\n\nGiven that $E[X] = \\frac{11}{20}$, compute $E[2X - X^{3} + 1]$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $E[aX + bY] = aE[X] + bE[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b$.*" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186157606 1074 "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{4})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]=\\frac{3}{10}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{20}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{10}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165166 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165167 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165168 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165169 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165170 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478816 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478834 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478884 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478920 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186165165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478952 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186165165 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478834 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186166748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479065 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [1759