y\\le 3\\}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186198651 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186198663 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{7}Mr\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot (\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (-0+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{31M}{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31M}{35}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31}{35}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186198663 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{7}Mr\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot (\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (-0+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{31M}{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31M}{35}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31}{35}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186230322 1078 #edb/ref 17592186230326 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186231576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186231576 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 8\\cos (4t) - 6\\sin (4t)$.\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos (4t + ϕ) + \\frac{3}{4}t\\cos (4t) + t\\sin (4t)$\n2. $y = R\\sin (4t + ϕ) + \\frac{3}{4}t\\cos (4t) + t\\sin (4t)$\n3. $y = A\\cos (4t) + B\\sin (4t) + \\frac{3}{4}t\\cos (4t) + t\\sin (4t)$\n\nNote that $A$, $B$, $R$, and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186231576 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186231576 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $D=1.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186233131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478985 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186233131 1071 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(4).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ |" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186233131 1071 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ |" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186233131 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=1$ and ${f}^{'}(1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186233131 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186233133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478974 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186233133 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=3x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2).$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186233133 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=3x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 true] [17592186233133 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3x+\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2-3x \\\\ x & =4-12x+9{x}^{2} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-13x+4 & =0 \\\\ (9x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{9},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChecking the solutions, we notice that only $x=\\frac{4}{9}$ satisfies the equation $3x+\\sqrt{x}=2.$ Therefore, we discard $x=1$ and conclude that ${f}^{-1}(2)=\\frac{4}{9}.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3x+\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{9})}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535185 17592186045417 false] [17592186233133 